course: dictionary
lesson: 2206
title: Breaking a big claim
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: A claim says something is true. Small claims, like «Some dogs are black», are easy to believe. Big claims use words like all, every, always, never and nobody. «All prime numbers are odd» is a big claim.
explain: To test a big claim, look for a counterexample: one single case that breaks it. The number 2 is prime and it is even, so it is a counterexample to «All prime numbers are odd». One counterexample is enough to prove the claim false.
explain: Examples that fit a claim cannot prove it true. Finding many odd primes does not prove that all primes are odd, because the next one might break the rule. Only a search for a counterexample tests a big claim properly.
fact: A counterexample is a single case that shows a general claim is false.
fact: Counterexample is a noun: a counterexample, counterexamples.
fact: Counterexamples work best on claims with «all», «every», «always», «never» or «nobody».
fact: One counterexample is enough to prove a general statement wrong.
fact: Many examples that fit a claim can make it look likely, but they cannot prove it true.
exercise: «All prime numbers are odd.» What is a counterexample to this claim? | *2 is prime and even | 3 is prime and odd | 9 is odd | 7 is prime and odd
exercise: «Everyone in our class likes football.» Sam says, «Well, I don't.» What does Sam's answer do? | *It is a counterexample that shows the claim is false | It proves everyone likes football | It is an example that proves the claim true | It is a question about football
exercise: Which word in «Cats never swim.» makes it a big claim? | *never | cats | swim | Cats
test: «Every cake in the shop has chocolate.» Tom finds a plain vanilla cake. What is the vanilla cake? | A proof that every cake has chocolate | A cake with no name | *A counterexample that shows the claim is false | A question about cakes
test: What is the best way to test «All birds can fly»? | Read a book about the sky | Ask a friend who likes birds | *Look for one bird that cannot fly, like a penguin | Count a hundred birds that can fly
exam: Which one is a counterexample to «Nobody in this school has ever been late»? | Everyone was on time today | *Sam was late once last week | The bell rang at nine | The school has a big hall
